  which framing is actually the one the paper's claim rests on.

==============================================================================
(C) MINIMAL, Session-1/2-realistic probe budget: 2 TE rings only
==============================================================================
Session 1/2's original motivating claim ('two same-type TE ring probes are
badly conditioned for angle') used exactly 2 ring probes (4 rows: neff+ng x 2
widths), NOT the 15-row all-5-widths-plus-MZI set used in (A)/(B) above.
That large-budget set is what Session 3 onward has used for every headline
Fisher number (86x, 91x, 571x) because it isolates a structure's MARGINAL
contribution against a realistic full baseline -- but it is a much bigger
probe budget than a real PCM unit cell (1-2 monitors) would deploy, and it is
NOT the setup Session 1's original degeneracy claim was about. Both framings
are reported because they answer different questions -- this section answers
the sharper, more paper-relevant one: does ONE additional rib/slot probe fix
the SPECIFIC minimal-budget degeneracy Session 1 found?

Using Session 2's reported best-conditioned strip pair: TE@550nm + TE@600nm
  strip TE@550+TE@600 alone: lambda_min=1.436e-04  cond=9.722e+09  angle std=21.021 deg
  full marginal std (w[nm],t[nm],a[deg]) = [8.07693369e+01 4.67448441e-02 2.10211325e+01]
  worst-eigenvalue eigenvector (w,t,a) = [-9.67760756e-01  5.45327357e-04 -2.51870646e-01] (eigval=0.00)
  -> MECHANISM: the worst (lambda_min) direction is dominated by WIDTH (|-0.97| >> |-0.25|), not angle. This is exactly
  why the blended lambda_min metric looked catastrophic (6.33, cond 2.4e5) while
  the MARGINAL angle CRB alone is much better-behaved (0.19deg) -- confirming
  Task 3's own premise: lambda_min can be dominated by width/thickness axes
  this project already handles well, making it a poor stand-in for the
  angle-specific claim. This is the mechanistic justification for why Task 3
  asked for the angle-marginal metric specifically, not just aggregate lambda_min.

